---
import CopyCommand from "../../components/CopyCommand.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { installCommand, podmanInstallCommand } from "../../lib/site";
---
<DocsLayout title="Installation" description="Prepare a Linux VM, GitHub OAuth, Cloudflare Tunnel, and complete the guided MyPaaS installer.">
<section><p class="overline">Quick start</p><h2>Choose the container engine</h2><p>Automatic dependency preparation targets fresh Ubuntu or Debian hosts. Docker Engine is the default path; rootful Podman is supported through the Docker-compatible command and socket contract.</p><CopyCommand command={installCommand} label="Docker Engine" /><CopyCommand command={podmanInstallCommand} label="Podman" /></section>
<section><h2>1. Prepare the domain</h2><p>Choose the hostname for the MyPaaS dashboard, for example <code>mypaas.example.com</code>. Project routes use hostnames below the configured public domain, so prepare both the root route and a wildcard route.</p><pre><code>mypaas.example.com
*.mypaas.example.com</code></pre></section>
<section><h2>2. Create the GitHub OAuth App</h2><p>Open GitHub account settings, then Developer settings → OAuth Apps → New OAuth App. Vendor navigation labels can change; the values below are the MyPaaS contract.</p><table><tbody><tr><td><strong>Application name</strong></td><td>MyPaaS</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Homepage URL</strong></td><td><code>https://mypaas.example.com</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Authorization callback URL</strong></td><td><code>https://mypaas.example.com/api/auth/github/callback</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Keep the generated Client ID and Client Secret ready. The installer derives the callback above from <code>PUBLIC_DOMAIN</code> when no explicit override is supplied.</p><aside class="warning"><p>Do not commit the GitHub Client Secret to a repository or paste it into project environment variables.</p></aside></section>
<section><h2>3. Create the Cloudflare Tunnel</h2><p>Create a remotely managed Cloudflare Tunnel for the MyPaaS host and copy its tunnel token. Configure published hostname routes for the dashboard hostname and wildcard project hostname, both targeting the MyPaaS Caddy service over HTTP.</p><aside class="note"><p>MyPaaS runs its own <code>cloudflared</code> container using the tunnel token. Do not install a second permanent cloudflared service on the host for the same tunnel.</p></aside></section>
<section><h2>4. Run the bootstrap</h2><p>The bootstrap installs Git when required, checks out <code>main</code> into <code>~/MyPaas</code>, prepares the selected container-engine dependencies, and starts the browser installer. The wizard binds to <code>127.0.0.1:8787</code> and can use a temporary token-protected Quick Tunnel; SSH port forwarding remains the fallback.</p><pre><code>ssh -L 8787:127.0.0.1:8787 user@your-vm</code></pre></section>
<section><h2>5. Complete the installer</h2><p>The guided installer collects the public domain, owner GitHub email, GitHub Client ID and Client Secret, and Cloudflare Tunnel token. Production database, JWT, encryption, and metrics secrets are generated or entered during this step.</p><ol class="steps"><li>Confirm the public domain.</li><li>Enter the GitHub primary email that should become owner.</li><li>Paste the OAuth Client ID and Client Secret.</li><li>Paste the Cloudflare Tunnel token.</li><li>Review generated production secrets and submit the configuration.</li></ol></section>
<section><h2>6. What gets installed</h2><p>The production control plane runs PostgreSQL, the Go API, SvelteKit dashboard, Caddy, and cloudflared. When enabled and supported, the installer also installs the pinned <code>mypaas-statd</code> release as a host-native systemd service.</p></section>
<section><h2>7. Verify production</h2><pre><code>cd ~/MyPaas
bash scripts/verify-production.sh
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml ps</code></pre><p>Confirm the dashboard resolves, API readiness succeeds, Caddy control path is healthy, the configured statd socket is available when enabled, and both root and wildcard tunnel routes work.</p></section>
<section><h2>Moving an existing installation?</h2><p>Use the supported VM migration flow instead of changing container engines in place. See <a href="/docs/migration/">Migration</a>.</p></section>
</DocsLayout>
